-- Passage rg3-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('09ce43bc-1977-53a9-a1d2-1eb2e363a71c', 'rg3-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$From Tree to Syrup$q$, $q$Maple syrup comes from the sap of maple trees. Sap is a clear, watery juice inside the tree. Turning sap into syrup takes time and care.

The work starts in late winter. During this time, the nights are freezing, but the days are warmer. These changes in temperature make the sap flow up and down inside the tree. That is why farmers collect sap at this time of year.

First, a farmer drills a small hole in the trunk of a maple tree. Next, the farmer puts a little spout into the hole. Sap drips out of the spout into a bucket or a tube.

Then the sap is carried to a sugar house. There, it is boiled in a wide, flat pan. Sap is mostly water. As it boils, the water rises away as steam. Because so much water leaves, the sap becomes thick and sweet.

It takes about forty buckets of sap to make just one bucket of syrup. That is why real maple syrup costs more than many other syrups. Finally, the hot syrup is strained and poured into jars.$q$, 'draft')
