-- Passage rg3-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2f8815e6-d716-5753-bb45-d633a36411b3', 'rg3-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How Traffic Lights Keep Us Safe$q$, $q$Busy streets meet at places called intersections. Without a plan, cars could crash into each other there. Traffic lights give every driver a turn.

Most traffic lights have three colors. Green means go. Yellow means the light is about to change, so drivers should slow down. Red means stop. The colors always appear in the same order, so drivers know what comes next.

How does a light know when to change? Many lights use a timer. The timer gives each street a set amount of green time. Other lights have sensors in the road. A sensor can tell when a car is waiting. Then the light changes sooner, so drivers do not wait when no cars are coming the other way.

Traffic lights help people who walk, too. Many corners have a walk signal. A small picture of a walking person means it is safe to cross. Some signals also beep so that people who cannot see well know when to go.

The next time you are at a corner, watch the lights. You will see a careful plan at work.$q$, 'draft')
